SELECT '8e9a2140-c66a-b30d-ef2e-afb4ea37be8c', 1, 'You are the PGY1 assisting in the pre-admission clinic and theatre suite of a 30-bed regional hospital in South Australia''s Mid North, 2 hours by road from Adelaide, where the visiting surgeon runs a monthly list. A 55-year-old woman, weight 78 kg, with no drug allergies and no history of multi-resistant organisms, is booked for an elective open repair of an umbilical hernia with mesh under general anaesthesia tomorrow. The anaesthetist asks you to chart her surgical antibiotic prophylaxis on the medication chart so the theatre nurse can prepare it, and to note when it should be given.', 'What is the correct prophylactic antibiotic regimen?', '[{"id":"A","text":"Cefalexin 500 mg orally, given with breakfast on the morning of the surgery."},{"id":"B","text":"Cefazolin 2 g intravenously at skin incision, then 8-hourly for the next 24 hours."},{"id":"C","text":"Cefazolin 2 g intravenously, single dose, within 60 minutes before incision."},{"id":"D","text":"Cefazolin 2 g intravenously, single dose, in recovery after wound closure."}]'::jsonb, 'C', '{"A":"Oral cefalexin at breakfast gives unpredictable tissue levels at the time of incision and is not a recommended surgical prophylaxis regimen; intravenous administration timed to incision is required.","B":"Giving the first dose at incision is slightly late, and continuing for 24 hours offers no additional protection for a clean procedure while increasing adverse effects and resistance; a single dose is standard, with intraoperative redosing only for prolonged surgery or major blood loss.","C":"This is correct. For a clean procedure with mesh implantation, cefazolin 2 g intravenously (3 g if weight is 120 kg or more) is given as a single dose within 60 minutes before incision, usually at induction, so that tissue concentrations are adequate when the incision is made.","D":"Prophylaxis given after wound closure is ineffective; the antibiotic must be present in tissues at the time of contamination."}'::jsonb,
  'Surgical antibiotic prophylaxis is a single intravenous dose of cefazolin 2 g given within 60 minutes before skin incision, not an oral dose, a postoperative dose or a prolonged course.', 'On a visiting list the junior doctor often charts prophylaxis in advance; writing the dose, route and the instruction to give at induction on the chart, and confirming allergies and weight, prevents a missed or mistimed dose when the anaesthetist is also managing other cases.', 'Surgery and perioperative care', ARRAY['Prescribing and medication safety','Infection and sepsis','Anaesthetics']::text[], ARRAY['1.8','1.1']::text[],
  'wound infection', 'theatre', 'recall', '{"age_band":"45-64","sex":"female","indigenous":false}'::jsonb, 3,
  'national', 'ai', NULL, '[{"source":"etg","title":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic","locator":"Surgical antibiotic prophylaxis: principles (timing, dosing, duration); hernia repair","supports":"key"},{"source":"acsqhc","title":"ACSQHC Surgical Antimicrobial Prophylaxis Clinical Care Standard (2020)","locator":"Timing of administration; single dose; weight-based dosing","supports":"key"}]'::jsonb,
  'Key facts: cefazolin 2 g IV within 60 minutes before incision (3 g if 120 kg or more); redose intraoperatively after about 4 hours or major blood loss; no postoperative continuation for most procedures; vancomycin for MRSA colonisation or severe beta-lactam allergy requires earlier infusion. Misconceptions: oral prophylaxis, prolonged courses. Out of scope: prophylaxis choices for contaminated procedures.', 'none', ARRAY[]::text[], NULL, NULL,
  'PGY1', ARRAY['D']::text[], 'rural'
  
